From e986fe252261d36f3cd34b713dc2b4953ff74a64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abhishek21lift-oss <abhishekkatiyar210399@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 13:35:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Gate every staff-only router behind requireStaff
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

requireStaff went in for /api/pt-os and stopped there. Its own comment in
middleware/rbac.js states why it exists: read routes were gated on `auth`
alone, "survivable only because no account had ever held the `member` role...
client logins create those accounts by the hundred." Ten other routers still
have that shape.

This is not a cross-tenant issue. tenantScope() confines everything to one
studio and that boundary is intact. It is a privilege one: a logged-in CLIENT
carries their studio's organization_id, so `auth` alone let them read their own
gym's staff data.

Concretely, from a client-portal login:

  GET /api/clients            `SELECT c.*` for up to 1000 pt_clients rows —
                              names, mobiles, emails, balances, notes, injuries.
                              The trainer scope in that handler only fires for
                              role === 'trainer', so a member skips it.
  GET /api/reports/revenue    the studio's total revenue.
  GET /api/reports/dues       the top 100 debtors and what they owe.
  GET /api/progress/*         nine GETs whose client_id is OPTIONAL; omitted,
                              each returns every row in the organisation.

plus trainers, payments, attendance, expenses, invoices, communication and
search on the same footing.

Verified before gating: the client portal calls exactly ONE endpoint, /api/me.
No screen under app/(bare)/member, /client or /member-login in
619-erp-frontend references any router touched here, so nothing client-facing
breaks. That check is what the earlier single-router version of this change had
to leave open.

requireStaff deliberately precedes the feature gate, so a client cannot learn
which features a studio has from the shape of the refusal. auth() runs twice on
those lines and that is fine — the second call is a user-cache hit, as the
/api/pt-os mounts already note.

The test asserts the MOUNTS rather than the handlers, because the mount is
where the fix lives and where a refactor would silently drop it, and spells out
the failing shape so a reviewer can see what regressed if it goes red.

Supersedes the /api/progress-only change; this covers it and nine more.

35 new tests. The 14 suites that read server.js and can run without a live
Postgres go from 138 passing to 173, with no regressions.

